      "publication_year": 2018,
      "publication_date": "2018-10-17",
      "abstract": "This paper defends the concept of racialization against its critics. As the concept has become increasingly popular, questions about its meaning and value have been raised, and a backlash against its use has occurred. I argue that when \u201cracialization\u201d is properly understood, criticisms of the concept are unsuccessful. I defend a definition of racialization and identify its companion concept, \u201cracialized group.\u201d Racialization is often used as a synonym for \u201cracial formation.\u201d I argue that this is a mistake. Racial formation theory is committed to racial ontology, but racialization is best understood as the process through which racialized \u2013 rather than racial \u2013 groups are formed. \u201cRacialization\u201d plays a unique role in the conceptual landscape, and it is a key concept for race eliminativists and anti-realists about race.",
